The routine lnx-s computes the natural logarithm of a whole number from 1 to 999, by pseudo-division, three-digit
remainder.

Input: x from 1 to 999. Result: 1000 ln x rounded, from 0 to 6908, in the accumulator (it does not fit a cell: STO
keeps the last three digits and SFT 03 leaves the thousands).

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 19. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 19-98 (80 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 67-69, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 252 instructions a call, 119.9 on average over the domain, counted from its first cell to its return
jump. Worst error: 2 in the last digit (thousandths) against the correctly rounded value, at x = 19; 405 of
the 999 inputs are off.
To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its instructions that points into
cells 19-98; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are. It holds no address bases.

